// @check
// @error a struct's fields are not a sequence, so `for … in P` has nothing to iterate — use a map (`{[string]: i32}`) for keyed iteration, or read the fields by name
// @error a `for` range counts in i32, so f64 and f64 bounds have no step — count with an integer and scale: `for i in 0 to 10 { const x = i / 10.0 }`
// @error a `for` range counts in i32, so a f64 step has no iteration count — count with an integer and scale: `for i in 0 to 10 { const x = i / 10.0 }`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `p` is inferred as `{a: i32, b: string}`
// The two `for` shapes that stay refused because the language has not ruled them, each naming
// the form that works instead of the type it wanted. A STRUCT's fields have different types,
// so a loop variable over one has no type to bind and the field read the author wants needs a
// key no operation accepts (`open-rulings.md` §B8-for-struct). A FLOAT range has no step: the
// inherited `+1` runs `0.0 to 1.0` once, and an explicit float step has no iteration count
// until the lowering is chosen (§B8-for-float-range).
type P = { a: i32, b: string }
const p: P = { a: 1, b: "x" }

for k in p { print(k) }

for x in 0.0 to 1.0 { print(x) }

for y in 0 to 10 step 0.5 { print(y) }
